{
  "evidence": "TEXT_D: Leave the EU, end freedom of movement, Australian-style points system, five-year moratorium on unskilled migration, and limits on skilled visas; five-year qualifying period for most benefits and non-urgent NHS access; restrict access to social housing and Help to Buy to long‑term taxpayers/citizens. TEXT_D: Spend at least 2% of GDP on defence, retain the nuclear deterrent, add 3,500 frontline police/prison staff, deport foreign offenders, confirm a UK Bill of Rights, withdraw from EU justice measures. TEXT_D: Raise personal allowance and 40% threshold, abolish inheritance tax, cut small business rates, penalise late payers, crack down on welfare fraud (proportionality/merit). TEXT_G: Remain engaged in the EU, reduce nuclear patrol posture, protect human rights law, Digital Bill of Rights, oppose blanket surveillance, replace elected police commissioners with boards. TEXT_G: Faster asylum decisions and allow work after six months; maintain 0.7% GNI aid; internationalist climate and environmental commitments. TEXT_G: NHS +£8bn, mental‑health waiting‑time standards, carers bonus; decriminalisation approach to drug possession. TEXT_G: Restrict non‑dom status, supplemental bank tax, banded levy on high‑value homes.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate profile prioritises Authority, Loyalty, and Proportionality, which are strongly reflected in TEXT_D’s emphasis on national sovereignty, border enforcement, defence spending, law‑and‑order measures, and contributor‑focused eligibility for benefits and housing. TEXT_G leans more toward cosmopolitanism, civil‑liberties constraints on state authority, and equality/care policies, making it a weaker fit with the profile’s top priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
